Per-binary method dispatch table (new; not an upstream backport).

Nim builds a method's dispatcher as an if-chain in the main module's C, after
every module is semmed. Which body a dynamic call runs is therefore decided by
the whole program: an override may sit in a module the caller cannot see (it
only has to be imported somewhere), and two modules may declare the same
signature, in which case the dispatcher silently keeps one. No macro or
compile-time view of a method can list the overrides that really run.

This patch makes the compiler say so. The main module's C gains, for an executable,

    void* nimMethodDispatchTable(NI* count);

which stores the row count in *count and returns a static const array of
    { void* bucket; void* fn; char const* recv; }
one row per branch of every dispatcher, in the dispatcher's if-chain order
(most specific receiver first): `bucket` is the address of the bucket's
dispatcher, `fn` the address of the method body that branch calls, `recv` the
name of the branch's receiver object type. The first row of a bucket whose
receiver the dynamic type inherits from is the body that runs. *count is -1
when dispatch is not an if-chain (`--experimental:vtables` under arc/orc), in
which case there are no rows.

`defined(nimHasMethodDispatchTable)` announces the accessor, as the other
`nimHas...` symbols announce theirs, so code written against it still compiles
with a stock compiler. The rows are static const data and the accessor a
constant function: nothing runs unless the program calls it. It is written for
the main module on the c and cpp backends (NIM_EXTERNC N_CDECL); no
platform-specific code is involved, so mingw and msvc take the same path.

It is written for executables only, never under --app:lib or --app:staticlib:
an exported symbol in every library would collide as soon as two Nim libraries
(or a Nim library and a Nim executable) were linked into one program. The
accessor also has hidden visibility (N_LIB_PRIVATE), since only code in the
same binary calls it. A library build therefore has no table, and a consumer
that imports the symbol must guard on the application type as well as on
`defined(nimHasMethodDispatchTable)`.

Consumer: nelli's re-executed replay child (RFC-0016 PRC-02b) reads the table
to confirm that every method body a dispatcher can run was one its footprint
scan read.

Repro: tests/method_dispatch_table.nim (the executable has the table) and
tests/method_dispatch_table_lib/run.sh (two static libraries link together).

--- a/compiler/cgen.nim
+++ b/compiler/cgen.nim
@@ -2356,6 +2356,51 @@
   let mm = m
   m.g.modulesClosed.add mm
 
+proc genMethodDispatchTable*(graph: ModuleGraph; m: BModule) =
+  ## A per-binary table of what each method dispatcher can run, and an
+  ## accessor for it, so a program (a re-executed replay child) can ask which
+  ## body a dispatcher would choose instead of guessing the override set: an
+  ## override may sit in a module the asker cannot see, and two modules may
+  ## declare the same override (the dispatcher silently picks one).
+  ##
+  ##   void* nimMethodDispatchTable(NI* count)
+  ##
+  ## returns `count` rows of {bucket, fn, recv}: `bucket` is the address of the
+  ## bucket's dispatcher, `fn` the address of the method body one branch of it
+  ## calls, `recv` the NUL-terminated name of that branch's receiver type. Rows
+  ## are in the dispatcher's if-chain order, so the first row of a bucket whose
+  ## receiver the dynamic type inherits from is the body that runs. `count` is
+  ## -1 when dispatch is not an if-chain (`--experimental:vtables`).
+  ## The rows are static const data. Main module of an executable only: a
+  ## static or dynamic library would export a symbol every other Nim binary
+  ## linking it also defines, and the accessor is called only by code in the
+  ## same binary, so it has hidden visibility.
+  if sfMainModule notin m.module.flags: return
+  let conf = m.config
+  if {optGenDynLib, optGenStaticLib} * conf.globalOptions != {}: return
+  let chain = optMultiMethods in conf.globalOptions or
+      conf.selectedGC notin {gcArc, gcAtomicArc, gcOrc} or
+      vtables notin conf.features
+  var rows = ""
+  var count = 0
+  if chain:
+    for bucket in 0..<graph.methods.len:
+      let disp = graph.methods[bucket].dispatcher
+      if disp.loc.snippet == "": continue
+      for meth in graph.methods[bucket].methods:
+        genProc(m, meth)
+        if meth.loc.snippet == "": continue
+        let recv = typeToString(meth.typ.firstParamType.skipTypes(skipPtrs))
+        rows.add "\t{(void*)&" & disp.loc.snippet & ", (void*)&" &
+                 meth.loc.snippet & ", " & $makeCString(recv) & "},\n"
+        inc count
+  m.s[cfsProcs].add "\nstatic NIM_CONST struct { void* bucket; void* fn; " &
+    "char const* recv; } nimMethodDispatchRows[] = {\n" & rows &
+    "\t{0, 0, 0}\n};\n" &
+    "NIM_EXTERNC N_LIB_PRIVATE N_CDECL(void*, nimMethodDispatchTable)(NI* count) {\n" &
+    "\t*count = " & $(if chain: count else: -1) & ";\n" &
+    "\treturn (void*)nimMethodDispatchRows;\n}\n"
+
 proc genForwardedProcs(g: BModuleList) =
   # Forward declared proc:s lack bodies when first encountered, so they're given
   # a second pass here
